------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A Verkada security trailer is a towable mast with cameras, an LTE modem and a
GPS module. Fleet Ops needs to know where a trailer is right now, and how far
it has been dragged in the last ten minutes, so the dashboard can raise a
"trailer is being stolen" alert.

The GPS vendor gives you exactly one function:

    struct GpsFix {
        int      status;     // GPS_ERROR / GPS_NO_FIX / GPS_VALID
        double   lat, lon;   // degrees, WGS-84
        float    hdop;       // horizontal dilution of precision
        uint64_t timestamp;  // microseconds, same clock as gps_now_us()
    };

    struct GpsFix gps_wait_for_fix(void);

It BLOCKS until the module emits its next fix:

    sky visible    blocks 20..90 ms, returns GPS_VALID with a fresh position
    sky blocked    blocks the full receiver timeout, returns GPS_NO_FIX and
                   the payload fields are garbage
    module hiccup  returns GPS_ERROR immediately, payload is garbage

  The FIRST call returns a GPS_VALID fix immediately (the module has a fix
  cached from cold start).

  It is NOT thread-safe: static first-call flag, static dead-reckoned
  position, static PRNG. Exactly one thread may ever call it.

  Harness scaling, so the whole run fits in ~7 s: the real receiver timeout is
  1 s — the fake module in main.c uses 100 ms. The real module errors maybe
  once in 10000 calls; the fake raises it to 2 % so the path gets exercised.

A dozen application threads (the RTSP pipeline, the geofence evaluator, the
cloud uplink, the local web UI) all want the position. None of them can afford
to sit in a 100 ms vendor call. Wrap it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.
  Design and implement a non-blocking, thread-safe way to read the most recent
  VALID fix.

      int  gps_cache_init(void);
      void gps_cache_deinit(void);
      int  gps_get_last_fix(struct GpsFix *out);   /* 0 = filled, -1 = none yet */

  The whole difficulty is in the payload: struct GpsFix is 40 bytes. There is
  no single atomic store for it, so "publish the latest value" is no longer one
  line. A reader must get lat, lon, hdop and timestamp FROM THE SAME FIX —
  never last second's lat paired with this second's lon, because that point is
  in the wrong county and the geofence fires.

Part 2.
  Design and implement history queries over the last 10 minutes. Non-blocking
  and thread-safe as well.

      int    gps_fix_at(uint64_t t, struct GpsFix *out);
      double gps_distance_travelled(uint64_t t0, uint64_t t1);   /* metres */

  gps_fix_at(t) is the most recent VALID fix with timestamp <= t. (Nothing
  after it means the trailer had not moved, or the module would have told us.)

  gps_distance_travelled(t0, t1) is the path length over the recorded fixes:

      k0 = first recorded fix with timestamp >= t0
      k1 = last  recorded fix with timestamp <= t1
      result = sum of segment_m(fix[k], fix[k+1]) for k in [k0, k1)
      0.0 if fewer than two fixes fall inside the range   (no interpolation
                                                           to the endpoints)

  Segment distance is the equirectangular ("flat earth") approximation — the
  trailer covers a few km, not a great circle, and this is one cos and one
  sqrt instead of haversine, with no libm-dependent atan2:

      #define DEG2RAD   0.017453292519943295
      #define EARTH_R_M 6371008.8
      double dlat = (lat2 - lat1) * DEG2RAD;
      double dlon = (lon2 - lon1) * DEG2RAD;
      double latm = (lat1 + lat2) * 0.5 * DEG2RAD;
      double x    = dlon * cos(latm);
      segment_m   = EARTH_R_M * sqrt(dlat*dlat + x*x);

  Use exactly that formula; the harness computes its ground truth with it.

  The dashboard polls gps_distance_travelled() once a second for every trailer
  in the fleet, and there can be 6000 fixes in the window. A range query that
  walks the range is O(n) — get it to O(log n).

  Return conventions (the harness enforces them):
      gps_fix_at              -1 if t is in the future, older than the window,
                              or before the oldest fix you hold
      gps_distance_travelled  NAN if t0 > t1, t1 is in the future, or t0 is
                              older than the window;  0.0 if t0 == t1

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour  (what main.c prints PASS/FAIL for)

  Part 1   a fix is available the instant gps_cache_init() returns, and it is
           field-for-field the fix the module emitted; same again after a
           second of sampling.
  Part 2   gps_fix_at() at a fix's own timestamp, between two fixes, and at
           "now"; gps_distance_travelled() over the whole path, each half, and
           halves-sum-to-whole, all against ground truth the fake module
           recorded (tolerance 1e-6, since the two sides add in a different
           order).
  Bounds   t in the future / older than the window / before the first fix;
           t0 == t1; t0 > t1; a range holding exactly one fix; a range
           entirely before the first fix.
  Safety   4 reader threads for 2 s while the sampler writes. The fake module
           only emits fixes where lon == -(lat + 85.0) and
           hdop == (float)(1.0 + 100.0*(lat - 37.0)), so ANY torn struct is
           caught by arithmetic; (timestamp, lat) is also checked against the
           recorded set, which catches a torn 64-bit timestamp.
  Latency  three getter calls in a row, timed. Fewer than 0.1 % of batches may
           exceed 5 ms (a loaded laptop gets one hiccup, a blocking getter is
           slow every time), the absolute worst must stay under one vendor
           block, and the readers must clear 10000 iterations in 2 s. The
           reference runs ~1 us per batch and ~1.6 M iterations.
  Shutdown after gps_cache_deinit() the module is no longer read, and the
           getters are still safe to call.

  ./main.sh window rebuilds with a 1.5 s window and adds eviction checks,
  including the one at the window's edge (see question 7).

  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

